  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe are given an initial 560-point solution achieving C5 = 0.380891. We need to find a better solution with C5 < 0.3808. The solution is a step function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with total area 1 (integral=1). Our approach uses a multi-stage optimization strategy combining several key elements:\n\n1. We start by modifying the initial solution using frequent pattern-based transformations to escape local optima\n2. Then we use multi-scaled optimization to effectively explore different resolutions\n3. Finally, we employ an adaptive optimization process that sorts active patterns and applies local search to refine the solution\n\nIndependent of resolution, the core idea is to identify and accentuate regularly spaced ones by designating their neighbors as mostly zeros. This minimizes the correlation (overlap) at shifts corresponding to those patterns. The expected result is a solution with significantly lower maximum overlap.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h using n samples (n=560 initially, with others possible). The function is piecewise constant on intervals defined by samples. The optimization constraints are:\n1) 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2) sum(h) * (2/n) = 1 (total area constraint)\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC5 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx \n= max\u2096 [sum d\u0131\u015f\u0131 measure overlaps at shift k]\n\nThis is approximated in the discrete domain as the maximum value of np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')*(2/n)\n\nFor implementation, we use a combination:\n- surrogate optimization (gradient-based for smooth version)\n- active pattern selection\n- local refinement\n- resolution migration\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with initial_h_values (if available) and two perturbations:\n   \u2219 pd1 = 0.5 + 0.5*(initial_h - initial_h[::-1])  # frequency inversion\n   \u2219 pd2 = 0.5 + 0.5*(initial_h + initial_h[::-1] - 1)  # improved frequency inversion\n   - Keep best solution from all variations.\n\n2. For the main optimization phase (use smooth value and gradient if needed):\n   Pseudo-code for smoothed objective and gradient functions must be defined, which:\n   - Use the potential function updates\n   - Combine local and global search approaches\n   - Calculate relevant gradients for active patterns\n\n3. Active pattern selection:\n   - Identify coordinates with largest potential overlap\n   - Use a gradient-based optimizer (FISTA variant)\n   - Maintain acceptability of solutions to ensure h[i] remains in [0,1]\n\n4. Other operations:\n   - Repair function to ensure feasibility\n   - Projection after gradient steps\n   - Candidate acceptance\n\n5. Optimization cycle:\n   - Loop over iterations, calculating projection until feasible\n   - Make few gradient steps and crossover updates\n   - Update best candidate if a better solution is found\n\n6. Local search refinement:\n   - After main optimization, apply a Nelder-Mead style local search\n\n7. Resolution migration (only if needed for else cases):\n   - Down-scale or up-scale candidate if benefits appear\n   - But only triple-check with exact evaluator\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Primary variation:\n   - Active pattern sorting and gradient descent\n   - FISTA for alternating optimization\n\n2. Local search variants:\n   - Frequency inversion\n   - Phase-reversal mutation\n   - Adaptive crossover\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n1. Expect time for FISTA and local search to be about 90% of total\n2. Deadline for FISTA is half the total calculation time\n3. Keep history of best solutions and restart if necessary\n4. Always maintain feasibility of candidates\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- At each step, make tie-breaker decisions if multiple candidates have same C5\n- Use periodic repair if C5 history shows no improvement for multiple steps\n- Upon termination, verify feasibility:\n   \u2022 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n   \u2022 sum(h) is n/2 (allow small error)\n   \u2022 C5 is finite\n- The function must return (h, C5, n_points)\n- Final solution must be checked using np.correlate against goal.",
